  <!-- Subsection 2: Core Distinctions (Movement Decompression & Interreality) -->
  <div class="mt-10 lg:mt-14 space-y-8">
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-vs-blue-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">
        Where Virtual Sports Differ
      </h3>
      <p>
        Based on the dimensions above, Virtual Sports fulfill two dimensions so distinctly that it clearly distinguishes them from traditional sports and esports. To understand the unique physicality of virtual sports, we use the concept of <b>Movement (de)compression</b>. To understand the unique reality of virtual sports, we use the concept of <b>Physical-Digital Interreality</b>. Combined, they make up an activity that is not exactly sports, nor esports.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Dimensions List with SVGs -->
    <ul class="space-y-6 lg:space-y-8" role="list">
      
      <!-- Distinction 1: Movement Decompression -->
      <li class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200/80 bg-white shadow-xs flow-root lg:text-justify">
        <!-- Header: Title & Subtitle -->
        <div class="mb-4">
          <h4 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-vs-blue-900 tracking-tight">
            Movement Decompression
          </h4>
          <div class="mt-1 flex items-center flex-wrap gap-1.5 text-base sm:text-lg font-normal text-slate-500">
            <span>Based on dimension</span>
            <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-xs shadow-sm">
              4
            </span>
            <span class="font-medium text-slate-700">Physicality</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SVG Graphic (floated right on desktop, aligned with paragraph 1) -->
        <figure class="my-4 lg:my-0 lg:float-right lg:w-3/5   flex flex-col items-center justify-center lg:py-2 pt-0 bg-white">
          {% svg "movement-compression", 
                 "w-full h-auto max-h-[340px] select-none", 
                 "Diagram contrasting fine-motor compressed movement in esports with authentic gross-motor decompressed movement in virtual sports", 
                 { viewBox: "0 0 2254 1110" } %}
          <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500 text-center">
            Source: Fouquaert (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, p. 78).
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- Narrative Paragraphs (wrap around figure and expand full width below) -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Within esports, there is often a discussion and reluctance to consider it as sports because it is argued that esports "lack great physicality" (Jenny et al., <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00336297.2016.1144517">2017</a>, p. 15) and are "non-physical" (Parry &amp; Giesbrecht, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00948705.2023.2216883">2023</a>, p. 210). However, others like Naraine (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.18060/24812">2022</a>, p. 40) argue that "esports <i>is</i> physical" given that other (traditional) sports like shooting require high-valued fine-motoric movements.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          To better understand physicality in the context of (e)sports, it clearly becomes essential to look past non-physical versus physical, or fine-motoric versus gross-motoric.
          The concept of <b>movement (de)compression</b> by Hemmingsen (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/16138171.2023.2259176">2024</a>) provides a solution to that. In essence, it considers <b>physicality in relation to the movements required in the traditional sport and how well these are mimicked</b>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-0">
          In esports, movements are highly <em>compressed</em>, meaning that the original movements from the parent sport are in a sense reduced to different (but not inferior) physical movements (e.g. accurately pressing buttons). 
          Applying movement (de)compression to virtual sports makes clear that <b>esports and virtual sports are actually different in their physicality</b>. Compared to esports, virtual sports clearly demonstrate highly <em>decompressed</em> movements (i.e. little <em>compressed</em>), mimicking the traditional "parent" sport with great accuracy and sensitivity. For example, the TGL SoFi simulator tracks the path of a golf club with millimeter precision.
        </p>
      </li>

      <!-- Distinction 2: Physical-Digital Interreality -->
      <li class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200/80 bg-white shadow-xs flow-root lg:text-justify">
        <!-- Header: Title & Subtitle -->
        <div class="mb-4">
          <h4 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-vs-blue-900 tracking-tight">
            Physical-Digital Interreality
          </h4>
          <div class="mt-1 flex items-center flex-wrap gap-1.5 text-base sm:text-lg font-normal text-slate-500">
            <span>Based on dimension</span>
            <span class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-vs-blue-900 text-white font-bold text-xs shadow-sm">
              9
            </span>
            <span class="font-medium text-slate-700">Reality</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SVG Graphic (floated right on desktop, aligned with paragraph 1) -->
        <figure class="my-4 lg:my-0 lg:float-right lg:w-1/2 flex flex-col items-center justify-center lg:py-2 pt-0 bg-white">
          {% svg "physical-digital-interreality", 
                 "w-full h-auto max-h-[340px] select-none", 
                 "Diagram illustrating physical-digital interreality and algorithmic mediation of physiological output in virtual sports", 
                 { viewBox: "0 0 2328 1425" } %}
          <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500 text-center">
            Source: Fouquaert (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, p. 82).
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- Narrative Paragraphs (wrap around figure and expand full width below) -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg  leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Activities like virtual sports or esport involve a digital translation of performances (e.g. human input mediated though a game engine).
          In such instances, it is important to <b>recognise the potential impact of this digital translation</b>. 
          If the outcome includes elements beyond solely human input (e.g. game engine algorithms), the sporting activity and its play becomes a hybrid outcome of what happens in the actual world and what happens in the digital world.  
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg  leading-relaxed mb-4">
        This potential <b>impact was previously not recognised</b>, as virtual sports games were considered "simulations in sport" with "digital measurements <em>representing</em> the physical output of the athlete competing in the sport" (Parry &amp; Giesbrecht, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00948705.2023.2216883">2023</a>, p. 221). In fact, it was even argued that games do not "fundamentally change the nature of the sporting activity" (Parry &amp; Giesbrecht, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00948705.2023.2216883">2023</a>, p. 221).
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg  leading-relaxed mb-0">
          The concept of <b>physical-digital interreality</b> by Fouquaert (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, p. 79) <b>acknowledges this new hybrid reality</b> based on research on esports and based on evidence on virtual cycling by Westmattelmann et al. (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2022.868902">2022</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/07421222.2025.2455774">2025</a>).
         These studies put forward empirical evidence of how measured physical performance (input) is not the only element that translates (100%) to the digital competition outcome (output).
         In virtual sports, the outcome is also determined by virtual, non-real, features and mechanisms such as virtual drafting dynamics, latency, gamification elements, avatars.
          As a result, the outcome of a virtual sport depends on both human input and digital alteration of such input. This makes clear that <b>sports and virtual sports are actually different in their reality</b>. Combined with difference in Movement Decompression, virtual sports can simply not be considered as sports nor as esports, but presents a new distinct category. 
        </p>
      </li>

    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Subsection 3: Towards a Universal Definition of Virtual Sports -->
  <div class="mt-8  lg:mt-10 space-y-6">
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-vs-blue-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">
        Towards a Universal Definition of Virtual Sports
      </h3>
      <p>
        Virtual sports are enabled by <em>virtuality</em>—the use of digital measurement technology to record and compare performances across different setups of time, space, and visual fidelity. Synthesising these characteristics leads to a scientific definition:
      </p>

      <!-- Scientific Definition Blockquote -->
      <blockquote class="my-6 p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 border-l-4 border-vs-blue-900 text-vs-blue-950 shadow-xs relative">
        <p class="text-lg md:text-xl leading-relaxed italic font-serif">
          &ldquo;Virtual Sports are institutionalised, rule-governed contests of human physical and cognitive skills that operate within a distinct physical-digital interreality that are characterised by voluntary human participation, a niche following, and high movement decompression mimicking a traditional parent sport, made possible through virtuality yet unbound to specific configurations of remoteness, (a)synchrony or representational fidelity.&rdquo;
        </p>
        <footer class="mt-4 not-italic text-sm sm:text-base font-semibold text-slate-600 flex items-center gap-2 font-sans">
          <span class="w-6 h-0.5 bg-vs-yellow-500 rounded-full inline-block"></span>
          <span>&mdash; <strong class="text-vs-blue-900 font-bold">Fouquaert (<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="hover:bg-vs-blue-100/30 text-vs-blue-700 underline decoration-slate-300 hover:decoration-vs-blue-700 transition-colors" href="https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/01KWESKD8T405VTFE3PADRE2JB">2026</a>, p. 84)</strong></span>
        </footer>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        This definition provides a precise foundation for scientific inquiry and sport governance. To support the sector's rapid growth, the Virtual Sports Research Network actively collaborates with industry partners, platform developers, and sports federations to translate this academic framework into accessible, industry-wide standards.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>